  "OPW fuel tank monitor BACnet"
 ],
 "equipment_type": "Other equipment (fuel tank gauge via BACnet gateway)",
 "applies_to": "OPW automatic tank gauge (ATG) consoles read by the Chipkin CAS-2700-40 gateway over the Veeder-Root TLS protocol (serial or TCP/IP).",
 "summary": "The Chipkin OPW gateway polls an OPW ATG console with the Veeder-Root TLS protocol and serves the tank data on BACnet IP (and Modbus and a web page). Each tank gets 9 Analog Inputs (volume, compensated volume, ullage, product and water height, temperature, status, product code) and 32 alarm / warning Binary Inputs. The printed example has four tanks; tank n uses instances (n-1) x 100 + 1 to 9 and + 10 to 41.",
 "bacnet_transport": [
  "BACnet IP"
 ],
 "firmware": null,
 "default_device_instance": 389001,
 "access_inferred": true,
 "notes": [
  "From the example BACnet object list in section 5.3.2 (default configuration, four tanks). The list is built from the tanks, sensors and options set up in the gateway, so a real gateway can differ.",
  "Sensor, vacuum sensor, relay, delivery and system alarm objects appear only when enabled; they are not in the printed example.",
  "Tank names come from the suffix entered for each tank; Tank01-Tank04 are the defaults shown.",
  "Volumes in US gallons, heights in inches and temperature in °F as printed; the gateway can be set to metric.",
  "There is no access column; all objects are read-only inputs."
 ],
 "source": {
  "title": "Chipkin OPW Gateway User Manual (CAS-2700-40)",
  "doc_number": "CAS-2700-40",
  "revision_date": "Rev 1, 2018-02-13",
  "url": "https://cdn.chipkin.com/assets/uploads/2020/Jan/CAS_MAN_CAS-2700-40_OPW-Gateway_Manual_28-16-56-53.pdf"
 },
 "integration": {
  "summary": "Connect the gateway to the console (RS-232 or TCP/IP), enter each tank address and suffix on the configuration page, then read volume, ullage, water and the common alarm for each tank over BACnet IP.",
  "minimum_points": [
   {
    "ref": "AI 3",
    "name": "Tank01_tank_volume",
    "why": "Tank 1 product volume, gal"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AI 5",
    "name": "Tank01_tank_ullage",
    "why": "Tank 1 ullage, gal"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AI 6",
    "name": "Tank01_tank_height",
    "why": "Tank 1 product height, in"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AI 7",
    "name": "Tank01_tank_water",
    "why": "Tank 1 water height, in"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AI 8",
    "name": "Tank01_tank_temperature",
    "why": "Tank 1 product temperature, °F"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AI 2",
    "name": "Tank01_tank_tank_status",
    "why": "Tank 1 status code"
   },
   {
    "ref": "BI 10",
    "name": "Tank01_tank_common_alarm",
    "why": "Tank 1 common alarm"
   }
  ],
  "enable": null,
  "heartbeat": "If the console stops answering, the gateway waits the Disconnect Time (default 120 s) and then sets every value to the configured Default Value; BACnet objects also go Out_Of_Service while the last good value stays in Present_Value.",
  "remote_sensors": null,
  "setup": [
   "Default BACnet device instance 389001, UDP port 47808; give each gateway a unique instance.",
   "Console link: Veeder-Root TLS protocol over RS-232 (port 1) or TCP/IP. Tank addresses are two digits (01-09).",
   "Up to 10 tanks, 10 sensors, 2 vacuum sensors and 10 relays on the standard page; more need the advanced page.",
   "Default scan interval 10 s, timeout 3 s, 3 retries."
  ],
  "gotchas": [
   "Instances depend on the tank order in the configuration; check the gateway Reports page for the live list.",
   "Pick a Default Value that cannot be mistaken for a real reading, so a lost console is obvious."
  ]
 },
